{"content":"Found the bug: in `Statement.token_first` (sqlparse/sql.py), the `matcher` function used with `_token_matching` was missing the `not` negation present in the analogous `token_next` matcher. `_token_matching` returns the first token for which `func(token)` is `True`, so the matcher must return `True` for the token that should be returned (i.e., the first non-whitespace/non-comment token), not for tokens to be skipped. The current code did the opposite, causing `token_first` to return the first whitespace token instead of skipping it — hence `get_type()` saw a whitespace token as the "first" token and returned `'UNKNOWN'`.

Fixing by adding the missing `not`, matching the correct logic used in `token_next`.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"replace_text","arguments":{"path":"sqlparse/sql.py","old_text":"        # this on is inconsistent, using Comment instead of T.Comment...
        def matcher(tk):
            return ((skip_ws and tk.is_whitespace)
                        or (skip_cm and imt(tk, t=T.Comment, i=Comment)))
        return self._token_matching(matcher)[1]","new_text":"        # this on is inconsistent, using Comment instead of T.Comment...
